// @check
// @error operator `-`'s first parameter must be named `self`, got `z` — operator dispatch binds the LEFT operand to `self`, so this declaration could never fire
// @error operator `*`'s first parameter must be named `self`, got `me` — operator dispatch binds the LEFT operand to `self`, so this declaration could never fire
// @error operator `+`'s first parameter must be named `self`, got `lhs` — operator dispatch binds the LEFT operand to `self`, so this declaration could never fire
// @error operator `<`'s first parameter must be named `self`, got `a` — operator dispatch binds the LEFT operand to `self`, so this declaration could never fire
// GRADUATED from `silent-class-inventory` D471 — the FOURTH and last shape of the
// reject `error-equality-not-overloadable.vl` (D46), `error-operator-arity-not-binary.vl`
// (D444) and `index/operator-arity.vl` already carry, and the one that stayed silent
// after #2005 because it was filed as a design question rather than a mechanism.
//
// IT IS THE POLLUTION RULE, AND IT IS RECEIVER-INDEPENDENT. `opSelfFnTy` requires
// `p0.parName == "self"` and returns -1 BEFORE it consults any type — its own comment
// names this "the pollution rule". So unlike D425, whose answer depends on what `self`
// is annotated as, this one is inert at EVERY receiver, a struct one included: rename
// the parameter of a declaration that dispatches and it stops, printing the built-in's
// answer instead of its own body's. Measured over 760 grid cells
// (`scripts/silent-sweep/d471/opdeclgrid.py`): ZERO of the 380 `z`-named cells
// dispatch, against 60 that do the moment the parameter is renamed `self`.
//
// THE DESIGN QUESTION THE ROW LEFT OPEN ANSWERS ITSELF, and the answer is measured
// rather than argued. The worry was that refusing here decides `function -(z, b)` is
// not a legal ordinary FUNCTION either. It is not one: `-` is not an identifier, so
// `-(7, 1)` is a PARSE ERROR, and the quoted spelling `function "-"` mints the
// identical name. There is no call syntax for it at any arity, under any spelling. So
// the declaration is exactly "a name no reference could ever be written for" — the
// criterion the arity gate beside it already applies, the one `indexOpDeclName`
// already applies to `"[]"`/`"[]="` (where a non-`self` first parameter has always
// been a hard parse error), and the one this file already applies to a quoted
// NON-operator (`function "shout"`). Refusing takes away nothing that can be written.
//
// SYNTACTIC, so it lives in `parseFuncTail` beside D444's arity clause rather than at
// the checker's hoist: the rule is about the parameter's NAME and the parser holds it.
// D425's twin — an arity-2 `self` whose TYPE can never be a receiver — needs the
// resolved type and lives at the hoist, and the two are disjoint by construction: a
// declaration that fails both gets this diagnostic only, which is the one that says
// what to change.
type V = { x: i32 }

function -(z: i32, b: i32): i32 { return 99 }

// Un-annotated is the same rule: the NAME is what is read, not the type.
function *(me, other) { 99 }

function "+"(lhs: V, other: V): i32 { return 99 }

// A STRUCT receiver does not rescue it — this is the whole point of "receiver-independent".
function <(a: V, b: V): boolean { return a.x < b.x }

// CONTROL — the identical declaration with the parameter named `self` DISPATCHES, and
// must keep dispatching. One character apart from the `<` above.
function >=(self: V, b: V): boolean { return self.x >= b.x }

function cell(p: V, q: V): boolean { p >= q }

print(cell({ x: 2 }, { x: 1 }))
